[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Checked vs. Unchecked Exceptions in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exceptions are a crucial part of Java's error-handling mechanism. They provide a way to gracefully handle unexpected events that occur during program execution. Java categorizes exceptions into two main types: checked and unchecked exceptions. Understanding the difference between these types is essential for writing robust and reliable Java code."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Checked Exceptions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Checked exceptions are exceptions that the compiler forces you to handle. If a method might throw a checked exception, you must either catch the exception using a try-catch block or declare that the method throws the exception using the 'throws' keyword in the method signature. This requirement ensures that potential exception scenarios are explicitly addressed in the code."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Checked exceptions are subclasses of the 'Exception' class, but not subclasses of the 'RuntimeException' class. Common examples include 'IOException', 'SQLException', and 'ClassNotFoundException'."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CheckedExceptionExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("nonexistent_file.txt"));
            String line = br.readLine();
            while (line != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
                line = br.readLine();
            }
            br.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("An IOException occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the 'FileReader' constructor and the 'readLine' method can throw an 'IOException', which is a checked exception. The code handles this exception using a try-catch block. If the file does not exist or if there's an error reading the file, the catch block will execute, printing an error message."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.IOException;

public class CheckedExceptionExampleThrows {

    public static void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(filename));
        String line = br.readLine();
        while (line != null) {
            System.out.println(line);
            line = br.readLine();
        }
        br.close();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            readFile("nonexistent_file.txt");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("An IOException occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the 'readFile' method declares that it throws an 'IOException' using the 'throws' keyword. The 'main' method then calls 'readFile' within a try-catch block to handle the potential exception. This approach shifts the responsibility of handling the exception to the calling method."},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"Unchecked Exceptions"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unchecked exceptions, also known as runtime exceptions, are exceptions that the compiler does not force you to handle. These exceptions typically indicate programming errors, such as null pointer dereferences, array index out-of-bounds errors, or illegal argument exceptions. While you can catch unchecked exceptions, it's generally considered best practice to prevent them through careful coding and validation."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unchecked exceptions are subclasses of the 'RuntimeException' class. Common examples include 'NullPointerException', 'ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException', and 'IllegalArgumentException'."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class UncheckedExceptionExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        try {
            int length = str.length(); // This will throw a NullPointerException
            System.out.println("Length: " + length);
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.err.println("A NullPointerException occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        int[] arr = new int[5];
        try {
            arr[10] = 42; // This will throw an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.err.println("An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, a 'NullPointerException' is thrown when trying to access the length of a null string, and an 'ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException' is thrown when trying to access an index outside the bounds of an array. While these exceptions are caught in the example, the preferred approach would be to prevent them by checking if the string is null before accessing its length and ensuring that array indices are within valid bounds."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Key Differences Summarized"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Checked Exceptions:</b> Must be caught or declared in the method signature using 'throws'."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Checked Exceptions:</b> Subclasses of 'Exception' (but not 'RuntimeException')."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Checked Exceptions:</b> Indicate potential problems that a well-written program should anticipate and recover from."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Unchecked Exceptions:</b> Do not need to be caught or declared."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Unchecked Exceptions:</b> Subclasses of 'RuntimeException'."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Unchecked Exceptions:</b> Typically indicate programming errors or invalid usage of APIs."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some best practices for working with checked and unchecked exceptions:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Handle Checked Exceptions:</b> Always handle checked exceptions appropriately, either by catching them or declaring them in the method signature. Choose the approach that makes the most sense for your application's design."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Prevent Unchecked Exceptions:</b> Focus on writing code that avoids unchecked exceptions in the first place. Use defensive programming techniques, such as null checks and input validation, to minimize the risk of these exceptions occurring."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Use Specific Exception Types:</b> Catch and throw specific exception types whenever possible. This makes it easier to understand the cause of the exception and handle it appropriately."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Document Exceptions:</b> Clearly document the exceptions that a method might throw, especially checked exceptions. This helps other developers understand how to use your code safely."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions and following these best practices, you can write more robust, reliable, and maintainable Java code."}]
